<%# A section's heading and standfirst, set the same way everywhere.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. `cards`, `catalogue` and `faq` each printed their own `h2` and `p` with their own classes,
  so the page's section rhythm depended on three partials agreeing by coincidence. They did not: the home
  page's three sections opened at the same size with the same gap, and nothing told a reader where one ended
  and the next began. One partial, one measure for the standfirst.

  THE RULE IS GONE AND THE BAND IS THE BOUNDARY. This used to draw a `border-t` above every heading, because
  a hairline was the only thing separating one section from the next in a single column of paper. Sections
  now alternate fields (shared/_sections.html.erb), so the boundary is a change of surface; keeping the rule
  as well would draw a line along a colour change that already reads as one.

  THE HEADING IS BIGGER THAN A CARD TITLE BY MORE THAN A STEP. It was `text-2xl sm:text-3xl` against an
  `h1` of `sm:text-5xl` and a body of `text-base` -- three sizes doing four jobs, which is why the page read
  as one long document at one scale.
%>
<% if heading.present? || lede.present? %>
  <div class="max-w-3xl">
    <% if heading.present? %>
      <h2 class="text-balance text-3xl font-bold leading-[1.25] tracking-tight text-lyn-ink sm:text-[2.5rem]">
        <%= heading %>
      </h2>
    <% end %>
    <% if lede.present? %>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-[1.8] text-lyn-ink-soft"><%= lede %></p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
<% end %>
